.Dd November 13, 2026
.Dt CRYPTO_EDDSA_CHECK_BATCH 3MONOCYPHER
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm crypto_eddsa_check_batch ,
.Nm crypto_eddsa_batch_workspace_size
.Nd batch verification of EdDSA signatures
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In monocypher.h
.Ft size_t
.Fo crypto_eddsa_batch_workspace_size
.Fa "size_t count"
.Fc
.Ft int
.Fo crypto_eddsa_check_batch
.Fa "const crypto_eddsa_batch_item *items"
.Fa "size_t count"
.Fa "const uint8_t (*coefficients)[16]"
.Fa "void *workspace"
.Fa "size_t workspace_size"
.Fc
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Fn crypto_eddsa_check_batch
checks many EdDSA (curve25519 + BLAKE2b) signatures at once,
faster than checking each of them with the single-signature
interface described in
.Xr crypto_eddsa_sign 3monocypher .
The signatures, public keys, and messages use exactly the same format
as the single-signature interface.
.Pp
Each item is described by a
.Vt crypto_eddsa_batch_item :
.Bd -literal -offset indent
typedef struct {
	const uint8_t *signature;   /* 64 bytes */
	const uint8_t *public_key;  /* 32 bytes */
	const uint8_t *message;     /* message_size bytes */
	size_t         message_size;
} crypto_eddsa_batch_item;
.Ed
.Pp
.Fa message
may be
.Dv NULL
only when
.Fa message_size
is 0.
Items may be duplicated:
each occurrence is checked as its own term of the aggregate equation.
.Ss The aggregate equation
Instead of checking each equation
[8](S B \- h A \- R) = 0 separately,
a single weighted aggregate is checked:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
/* [8] * sum_i( z_i * (S_i B - h_i A_i - R_i) ) = 0 */
.Ed
.Pp
As in the single-signature check,
h_i is BLAKE2b(R_i || A_i || message_i) reduced modulo the prime order L,
computed over the
.Em raw
signature and public key encodings.
Per-item rules are unchanged and are enforced for every item:
non-canonical A and R encodings are allowed,
points not on the curve are rejected,
and S must be below L.
Low-order components are eliminated by the final multiplication
by the cofactor (8).
The aggregate is evaluated as one multi-scalar multiplication over all
2\~N+1 terms (the base point term included),
with a 4-bit windowed bucket method whose point doublings are shared
by the whole batch.
.Ss Random coefficients (required)
The
.Fa coefficients
z_i are 128-bit little-endian integers, and must be non-zero.
They
.Em must
be drawn from a cryptographically secure random source,
independently of each other,
and only
.Em after
every signature, public key, and message of the batch is fixed.
If an attacker can influence the coefficients,
they can make a batch of invalid signatures pass.
Monocypher does not gather system entropy:
obtaining randomness is the caller's responsibility.
.Ss Probabilistic guarantee
Batch verification is probabilistic.
A successful check proves the aggregate equation holds for the
coefficients that were used;
a batch that contains invalid signatures still has a small chance
(about 1 in 2^128 per random choice of coefficients)
of being accepted.
Do not treat a batch success as a deterministic proof of each
individual signature.
Fixed, published coefficients are acceptable for reproducible
test runs only, never for security decisions.
.Ss Workspace
.Fn crypto_eddsa_check_batch
uses no heap allocation and keeps no state between calls;
its working memory is provided by the caller.
.Fn crypto_eddsa_batch_workspace_size
returns the number of bytes needed for
.Fa count
items.
It returns 0 when
.Fa count
is 0,
and
.Dv SIZE_MAX
if the size would overflow
.Vt size_t .
It performs no allocation.
The workspace must come from an allocation with fundamental alignment
(such as
.Xr malloc 3 )
and must not overlap the items, the coefficients, or any signature,
key, or message buffer.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
.Fn crypto_eddsa_check_batch
returns 0 if the aggregate equation holds,
\-1 if a point or signature encoding is rejected or the equation
does not hold,
and \-2 if the arguments or the workspace are invalid.
An empty batch
.Pq Fa count No = 0
always returns 0, and all pointers may be
.Dv NULL .
.Sh EXAMPLES
The following example assumes the existence of
.Fn arc4random_buf ,
which fills the given buffer with cryptographically secure random bytes.
.Bd -literal -offset indent
/* Check a batch of NB signatures */
const crypto_eddsa_batch_item *items; /* Filled elsewhere   */
size_t                         count; /* Number of items    */
uint8_t (*coefficients)[16] = malloc(count * sizeof *coefficients);
if (coefficients == NULL) { /* Handle error */ }
size_t workspace_size = crypto_eddsa_batch_workspace_size(count);
void *workspace = malloc(workspace_size);
if (workspace == NULL) { /* Handle error */ }

/* Fix the whole batch first, *then* draw random coefficients */
arc4random_buf(coefficients, count * sizeof *coefficients);
for (size_t i = 0; i < count; i++) {
	if (!memcmp(coefficients[i], (uint8_t[16]){0}, 16)) {
		coefficients[i][0] = 1; /* Zero is not allowed */
	}
}

int status = crypto_eddsa_check_batch(items, count,
                                      (const uint8_t (*)[16])coefficients,
                                      workspace, workspace_size);
if (status != 0) {
	/* Batch rejected: fall back to per-signature checks to
	 * find the culprit, or reject them all.               */
}
free(workspace);
free(coefficients);
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr crypto_eddsa_sign 3monocypher ,
.Xr crypto_blake2b 3monocypher ,
.Xr intro 3monocypher
.Sh STANDARDS
These functions check PureEdDSA signatures with Curve25519 and BLAKE2b,
as described in RFC 8032 (with BLAKE2b instead of SHA-512).
The weighted batch equation follows the standard
.Sq Bos-Coster
style aggregation with random 128-bit coefficients.
.Sh CAVEATS
.Sy Batch success is not a per-signature proof.
See
.Sx Probabilistic guarantee
above.
.Pp
These functions do not run in constant time.
This is not a problem for signature verification,
whose inputs are public.
